\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Linear changes of reference volume}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{Let #{T:X_V\to X_W} be an invertible real linear map, with
#{|V|=|W|=n}. Its
\vocabk{reference-volume Jacobian}{fdip-00JI} #{J(T)} is positive.
For Borel sets #{E,B},
##{\begin{aligned}
\lambda_W(T(E))&=J(T)\lambda_V(E),\\
T_*\lambda_V(B)&=J(T)^{-1}\lambda_W(B).
\end{aligned}}
For nonnegative Borel #{F:X_W\to[0,\infty]},
##{\int_{X_V}F(Tx)\,\extd\lambda_V(x)
 =J(T)^{-1}\int_{X_W}F(y)\,\extd\lambda_W(y).}
For real signed #{F}, absolute integrability on either side is equivalent
to that on the other; under this condition the same equality holds.
Translation, and hence #{x\mapsto T(x)+b}, has the same coefficient.}
\proof{
\p{Choose orderings #{v_1,\ldots,v_n} and #{w_1,\ldots,w_n}
of the carriers. Their indicator bases are orthonormal for the counting
inner products. Let #{A} represent #{T} in these two ordered bases.
The adjoint identity makes #{A^T} the array of #{T^\dagger}; thus
##{\det(T^\dagger T)=\det(A^TA)=(\det A)^2>0.}
The last inequality uses invertibility; for #{n=0} the determinant is
#{1}. Let #{\eta_V,\eta_W} be the top forms taking value one on the
chosen ordered bases, and write #{T^*\eta_W=d(T)\eta_V}.
Alternating multilinearity gives #{d(T)=\det A}, so
##{J(T)=\sqrt{\det(T^\dagger T)}=|\det A|=|d(T)|.}
Reordering either indicator basis changes #{d(T)} only by a sign.
The absolute coefficient therefore agrees with the intrinsic definition.}
\p{For an endomorphism, #{d(T)=\det T} only when source and target
use the same normalized top form. Independently ordered indicator bases
give #{d(T)=\varepsilon\det T}, #{\varepsilon\in\{1,-1\}}, although
#{J(T)=|\det T|}. The identity on #{\mathbb R^2} with reversed target
order has #{d=-1}, intrinsic determinant #{1}, and #{J=1}.
In dimension zero the top form is #{1} and #{J=1}.}
\p{Top exterior powers are one-dimensional. For composable maps #{T,S},
use the same intermediate form to obtain
#{(ST)^*\eta=d(S)d(T)\eta_V}. Thus #{J(ST)=J(S)J(T)};
applying this to #{T^{-1}T} gives #{J(T^{-1})=J(T)^{-1}}.
The absolute identities are independent of enumeration. Since #{T^{-1}}
is continuous, #{T(E)} is Borel.}
\p{On the Borel real line, translation preserves length and multiplication
by #{a\ne0} has pushforward coefficient #{|a|^{-1}}. Compare preimages
of bounded intervals and then use finite-on-bounded-interval uniqueness.
When #{a<0}, the reversed half-open interval has the same length since
singletons have measure zero. By rectangles and \ref{fdip-002I},
scaling one coordinate has that coefficient. Coordinate permutations
preserve measure by \ref{fdip-002J}. With a common basis their signed
top-form multipliers are #{a} for scaling and #{-1} for a swap.}
\p{For distinct coordinates #{i,j}, the shear
#{E(x)=x+c\,x(j)\delta_i} preserves volume. Split off coordinate #{i},
writing the others as #{z}. For fixed #{z}, translation invariance gives
##{\int_{\mathbb R}F(z,t+c\,z(j))\,\extd t
   =\int_{\mathbb R}F(z,t)\,\extd t.}
Nonnegative Tonelli on the sigma-finite uncompleted Borel product then
gives equality of full integrals; indicators give measure preservation.
The shear's top-form multiplier is #{1}, since replacing the #{j}th
basis vector by #{\delta_j+c\delta_i} leaves its top wedge unchanged.
Tonelli here is
\citet{Theorem 1.7.15, pp. 200-201, preliminary version}{tao2011measure},
not the complete-space variant.}
\p{These elementary maps generate every isomorphism in indicator charts.
Induct on #{n}, the zero-dimensional case being immediate. For #{n>0},
the image of the first basis vector is nonzero. Swap target coordinates
to put a nonzero coefficient first, scale it to one, and subtract its
suitable multiples from the other target coordinates. The resulting
map #{ET} sends the first domain basis vector to the first target one.
If the first coefficient of #{(ET)e_j} is #{a_j} for #{j>1}, precompose
with the domain shears #{e_j\mapsto e_j-a_je_1}. The resulting map
fixes the first basis vector and maps the complementary span into the
target complementary span. Its restriction is injective; it is surjective
because the first coefficient of a preimage of a complementary vector
must be zero. Induction on these complements, extending their elementary
maps by identity on the first coordinate, completes the factorization.}
\p{Pushforwards compose and commute with scalar multiplication of
measures. Multiplying the elementary coefficients therefore proves
#{T_*\lambda_V=J(T)^{-1}\lambda_W}, after the measure-preserving
coordinate charts of \ref{fdip-002J}. Substituting #{B=T(E)} gives
the image formula. Translations in several coordinates follow from the
one-dimensional result and rectangles, yielding the affine assertion.
No reciprocal formula is asserted for a singular map.}
\p{The integral formula follows first for indicators from the measure
identity, then for nonnegative simple functions by additivity, then by
increasing simple approximation and monotone convergence. Apply it to
#{|F|} to obtain absolute-integrability equivalence, since #{J(T)} is
positive and finite. Only then apply it to #{F^+,F^-} and subtract.
No multiplication closure of #{L^1} is used.}
}
\p{The image convention is stated in
\citet{Exercises 1.2.20-1.2.22, p. 41, preliminary version}{tao2011measure};
the argument above proves the formula for finite real field spaces.
Mathlib's
\leanref{Real.map_linearMap_volume_pi_eq_smul_volume_pi} is a
finite-coordinate endomorphism theorem with nonzero determinant and
reciprocal pushforward coefficient. Distinct carriers require the
coordinate transport already proved, not an intrinsic signed determinant
between unrelated spaces.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.normDet_sq} and
\leanref{LinearMap.normDet_nonneg} identify this square root with
#{\operatorname{normDet}(T)} for finite-dimensional real inner-product
spaces. Its \leanref{LinearMap.normDet_eq_norm_det_toMatrix} gives the
absolute determinant in two orthonormal bases with the same finite index
set. These are algebraic volume-factor identities; the Borel image and
integrability assertions are proved above.}

\p{Remark. The positive factor #{J(T)} is the absolute value of the
scalar by which #{T} acts on the top exterior power #{\Lambda^n}.
An orthogonal sum has product volume because
#{\Lambda^{\mathrm{top}}(U\oplus W)\simeq\Lambda^{\mathrm{top}}U\otimes\Lambda^{\mathrm{top}}W}.}
